[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Exit status differed when stdout was closed early (SIGPIPE)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With a subject long enough to fill the pipe buffer, and the reader
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m gone:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ c_src/build/driver "$(python3 -c "print('x'*100000)")" | head -c 10 >/dev/null
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m status 141          # killed by signal 13
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ translation/target/release/driver "$(...)" | head -c 10 >/dev/null
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m status 0            # exited normally
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout and stderr agreed; only the status differed, which is exactly the failure
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mode a stdout-only assertion misses.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs. The failing `write` therefore returned `EPIPE` instead of terminating the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m process, and `run()` discards write errors with `let _ = ...` (which is itself
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m correct — see "Deliberately preserved behaviour" below), so the program fell
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m through to `ExitCode::from(0)`. The C program keeps the default disposition and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is killed by signal 13 mid-`printf`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `src/main.rs` resets `SIGPIPE` to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SIG_DFL` as the first statement of `main`. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `stdout_closed_early_kills_both_programs_the_same_way`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Confirmed no other mismatch.** Every other input class below already agreed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Across a 7718-case shell/Python sweep and the 22 tests in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/differential.rs`, this was the only divergence observed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Branch enumeration
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each `if` and early `return` in `main.c`, the inputs that reach it, and the test
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that covers it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | # | C branch | Reaching input | Result | Test |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 1 | `argc == 1` | no arguments | usage, exit 1 | `argc_one_prints_usage` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 2 | `argc > 4` | 4+ operands | usage, exit 1 | `argc_above_four_prints_usage` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 3 | `argc == 2` | subject only | whole string | `single_argument_prints_whole_string`, `..._passes_bytes_through_unchanged` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 4 | `end == argv[2]` | `""`, `"abc"`, `" "`, `"+"`, `"-"`, `"\xff"`, … | `Second argument must be an integer!`, **no newline**, exit 1 | `second_argument_not_an_integer` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 5 | `start > len` | `6`, `100`, `-1`, `2147483648`, `9223372036854775807` | `start is off the end`, exit 1 | `start_off_the_end_of_the_string` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 6 | neither | `0`, `len-1`, `len` | substring (empty at `start == len`) | `start_at_the_boundaries_is_accepted`, `start_uses_strtol_parsing_rules` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 7 | `end == argv[3]` | **unreachable** | — | `third_argument_error_message_is_unreachable` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 8 | `stop > len` | `6`, `-1`, `2147483648`, `9223372036854775807` | `stop is off the end`, exit 1 | `stop_off_the_end_of_the_string` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 9 | `stop <= start` | `2 2`, `3 2`, `0 0`, and any unparsable third arg | `stop must come after start!`, exit 1 | `stop_must_come_after_start`, `non_integer_third_argument_falls_through_to_the_ordering_check` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 10 | `else stop = len` | `argc == 3` | stop defaults to end | `start_at_the_boundaries_is_accepted` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 11 | final `printf` | valid `[start, stop)` | substring + `\n` | `valid_ranges_print_the_substring`, `every_valid_range_of_a_short_string` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `every_valid_range_of_a_short_string` sweeps `start` and `stop` over
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `-2 ..= len+2` for subjects of length 0, 1, 2 and 8, so rows 5, 6, 8, 9 and 11
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m are each hit many times, including at every boundary.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Deliberately preserved behaviour
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These look like defects. The C is the ground truth, so they are reproduced, not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m repaired.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`Second argument must be an integer!` has no trailing newline**, unlike every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   other message. `$(...)` in a shell strips trailing newlines, so a naive harness
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   cannot see this; the tests compare raw bytes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`Third argument must be an integer!` is dead code.** `strtol(argv[3], NULL, 10)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   passes `NULL` for `endptr`, so `end` still holds the pointer from the `argv[2]`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   conversion. That pointer always lies within `[argv[2], argv[2]+strlen(argv[2])]`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and `argv[3] == argv[2] + strlen(argv[2]) + 1`, so `end == argv[3]` is off by at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   least one and can never hold. Consequence: an unparsable third argument yields
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `stop == 0` and is reported as `stop must come after start!` instead.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `third_argument_error_message_is_unreachable` sweeps 4x18x18 = 1296 inputs and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   asserts the C program never emits the message.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`start > len` / `stop > len` compare `int` against `size_t`.** The usual
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   arithmetic conversions widen the signed `int` to `unsigned long`, so `-1`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   becomes `ULONG_MAX` and negative indices are reported as "off the end" rather
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   than as invalid. Rust reproduces this with `(start as u64) > len`, relying on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `i32 as u64` sign-extending.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`long` -> `int` truncation.** `strtol` returns `long`; `start`/`stop` are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `int`. So `4294967298` acts as `2`, `4294967296` acts as `0`, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `2147483648` acts as `INT_MIN` (rejected as off the end). Rust uses `value as i32`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`strtol` saturation.** Out-of-range input yields `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `(int)LONG_MAX == -1` (off the end), while `(int)LONG_MIN == 0`, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `-9223372036854775808` is silently accepted as index 0. The hand-written
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `strtol` accumulates in the negative direction so `LONG_MIN` is representable,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and flags the `+2^63` case separately.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Write errors are ignored.** The C code never checks `printf`'s return value,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so a full or unwritable stdout still exits 0. Verified against `/dev/full` by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `unwritable_stdout_is_ignored_identically`. This is why `run()` uses
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `let _ = out.write_all(..)`; the SIGPIPE fix above addresses the status
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   difference without introducing error checking the C does not perform.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Bytes are passed through, not text.** `argv` may hold non-UTF-8. The Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   code reads `args_os()` and takes raw bytes via `OsStrExt`, never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `to_string_lossy`, so `\xff\xfe\xfd` round-trips.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Known-unreachable path (not a mismatch)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `else stop = len` truncates `size_t` to `int`. This would differ from a clamping
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m implementation only when `strlen(argv[1]) > INT_MAX`. Linux caps a single `argv`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m entry at `MAX_ARG_STRLEN` (32 pages); measured on this host, 131071 bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m succeed and 131072 fails with `E2BIG`. `len` therefore cannot approach
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `INT_MAX`, and the branch is unreachable through the process interface. Recorded
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m because mutation testing showed it is the one change to `src/main.rs` the suite
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cannot detect — by construction, not by omission.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
